# Removing Event Listeners

Just as we can add event listeners, we can remove them, to say that we no longer want a callback run when an event occurs.

The method is **`el.removeEventListener(event, callback)`**, and it mirrors `addEventListener` exactly:

```javascript
const btn = document.querySelector('button');

function callback() {
  console.log('clicked');
}

btn.addEventListener('click', callback);

// ...later...

btn.removeEventListener('click', callback);
```

Same element, same event name, same callback. After that last line, clicking the button does nothing.

## The Callback Has to Be the *Same* Function

This is the part that trips people up. `removeEventListener` finds the listener to remove by matching the **function object**, not by comparing what the function does.

So this does not work, even though the two functions are identical in behavior:

```javascript
function callback() {
  console.log('clicked');
}

function callback2() {
  console.log('clicked');
}

btn.addEventListener('click', callback);
btn.removeEventListener('click', callback2);   // does nothing
```

`callback` and `callback2` are two different function objects that happen to contain the same code. The browser is looking for the first one and does not find it.

And for the same reason, this does not work either:

```javascript
btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log('clicked');
});

btn.removeEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log('clicked');
});                                             // does nothing
```

Each of those arrow functions creates a brand new function object at the moment the line runs. They are not the same object, so nothing is removed.

**The practical consequence: if you might ever want to remove a listener, do not write its callback inline.** Give it a name, or store it in a variable, so you have something to hand to `removeEventListener` later.

You can watch the difference:

```html id=page1
<button id="removable">Named callback</button>
<button id="stuck">Inline callback</button>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const removable = document.querySelector('#removable');
const stuck = document.querySelector('#stuck');

function onRemovable() {
  console.log('removable listener fired');
}

removable.addEventListener('click', onRemovable);
removable.removeEventListener('click', onRemovable);

stuck.addEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log('inline listener fired');
});

stuck.removeEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log('inline listener fired');
});
```
```+id=page1
```

Click both. The first button is silent, because its listener really was removed. The second still logs, because the attempted removal referred to a different function.

## A Worked Example: Dragging

Here is a real use for removal. We want a block the user can drag around, which means the block should follow the pointer *only between* pressing the mouse down and releasing it.

The shape of the solution: `mousedown` adds a `mousemove` listener, and `mouseup` removes it.

```html id=page2
<div id="block"></div>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const block = document.querySelector('#block');

function onMouseMove(event) {
  block.style.left = event.pageX + 'px';
  block.style.top = event.pageY + 'px';
}

function onMouseDown() {
  window.addEventListener('mousemove', onMouseMove);
}

function onMouseUp() {
  window.removeEventListener('mousemove', onMouseMove);
}

block.addEventListener('mousedown', onMouseDown);
window.addEventListener('mouseup', onMouseUp);
```
```+id=page2
```
```+css
#block { width: 50px; height: 50px; background-color: red; position: absolute; }
```

Press the mouse down on the red square and drag. Release, and it stops following.

Two details in that code are worth pointing out.

**`position: absolute`** in the CSS is what lets us place the block anywhere on the page by setting `left` and `top`. Without it, those properties would have no effect.

**`mouseup` is on the `window`, not on the block.** That is deliberate. While dragging quickly, the block lags slightly behind the pointer, so when the user lets go, the pointer may not be over the block at all. A `mouseup` listener on the block would miss that release and the block would keep following the pointer forever. Listening on the window catches the release wherever it happens.

There is plenty this example does not handle. Most visibly, the block always jumps so its top-left corner is under the pointer, rather than staying where you grabbed it. The point here is the add-then-remove pattern, which is what makes a drag a drag.

## Exercises

1. Add a click listener and then remove it, so that clicking the button leaves `count` at `0`.

    ```html id=page3
    <button id="once">Click</button>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const button = document.querySelector('#once');

    let count = 0;

    function handleClick() {
      count = count + 1;
    }

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page3
    ```
    ```+solution
    const button = document.querySelector('#once');

    let count = 0;

    function handleClick() {
      count = count + 1;
    }

    button.addEventListener('click', handleClick);
    button.removeEventListener('click', handleClick);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(source.includes('addEventListener')).should('equal', true).run('Add the listener first', 'Added the listener');
    wrap(source.includes('removeEventListener')).should('equal', true).run('Then remove it with `removeEventListener`', 'Removed the listener');
    assert('#once').click().run('Could not find the `#once` button', 'Clicked the button');
    wrap(count).should('equal', 0).run('`count` should still be `0`, because the listener was removed before the click', 'The listener really was removed');
    ```

2. Make a button that works exactly once: the first click sets the message to `Claimed`, and every later click does nothing. Remove the listener from inside the callback.

    ```html id=page4
    <button id="claim">Claim your prize</button>
    <p id="message">Unclaimed</p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const button = document.querySelector('#claim');
    const message = document.querySelector('#message');

    let clicks = 0;

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page4
    ```
    ```+solution
    const button = document.querySelector('#claim');
    const message = document.querySelector('#message');

    let clicks = 0;

    function onClaim() {
      clicks = clicks + 1;
      message.textContent = 'Claimed';
      button.removeEventListener('click', onClaim);
    }

    button.addEventListener('click', onClaim);
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#claim').click().run('Could not find the `#claim` button', 'Clicked once');
    assert('#message').should('have.text', 'Claimed').run('The first click should set the message to `Claimed`', 'The first click works');
    wrap(clicks).should('equal', 1).run('The callback should have run once', 'The callback ran once');
    assert('#claim').click().click().run('Could not click again', 'Clicked twice more');
    wrap(clicks).should('equal', 1).run('Later clicks should do nothing. Call `removeEventListener` from inside the callback, passing the same named function', 'Later clicks are ignored');
    ```

3. You added a listener with an inline arrow function and now you need to remove it. What has to change?

    ```{mcq}
    `el.addEventListener('click', () => { ... });` needs to become removable. What do you do?
    correct: Correct! `removeEventListener` matches on the function object, so you need a reference to the exact function you passed in. Naming it, or storing it in a variable, gives you one.
    wrong: Revisit "The Callback Has to Be the Same Function", and why passing an identical-looking arrow function to `removeEventListener` does nothing.
    [x] Give the callback a name, or store it in a variable, and pass that to both calls
    [ ] Pass an identical arrow function to `removeEventListener`
    [ ] Call `removeEventListener` with only the event name
    [ ] Nothing; inline arrow listeners are removed automatically
    ```

